  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Abdulla K.K vs Food Inspector on 14 March, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 14Th Day Of March 2016/24Th Phalguna, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 465 of 2012 () ------------------------------- Crl.A 1044/2007 of Addl.Sessions Court, Vadakara Cc 539/2003 of J.M.F.C., Nadapuram Revision Petitioners/Appellants/Accused: ---------------------------------------- 1. Abdulla K.K. S/O. Soopy, Koroth Kandy House, P.O. Poolakool Via Kakkat 2. Ibrai Kottyil, S/O. Ammad P.O. Poolakkool, Via Kakkat (Pfa License Of The Shop Pv P Ix/301 A) By Advs.Sri.P.Sreekumar Sri.K.S.Manu (Punukkonnoor) Sri.P.S.Sidhan Sri.V.K.Rajanandan Respondent(S)/Respondents: ------------------------- 1. Food Inspector Perambra Circle, Kozhikod. 2. The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam - 682 031. Abdulla K.K vs Food Inspector on 14 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/163813017/ 1 R By Public Prosecutor Sri.V.S.Sreejith This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 14-03-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P.No. 465 of 2012 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 14th day of March, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "Heard. 3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 23.04.2003 at about 11 a.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 3 bottles of tomato sauce, each bottle contained 200 gram from the first accused. The second accused was the licensee of the shop and the third accused was the manufacturer. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The public analyst submitted Ext.P14 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for tomato sauce as per the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P28 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for tomato sauce as per the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts.P1 to P35 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 was examined for the revision petitioners. Abdulla K.K vs Food Inspector on 14 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/163813017/ 2 5.",
    "arguments": "The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P28 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext.P28 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P28 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext.P28 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
